{{also|płod|płód}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{rhymes|ɑːd|lang=en}}
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/plɒd/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-plod.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ɒd|lang=en}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} *{{m|enm||plodden}} (found only in derivative {{m|enm|plodder}}), probably originally a splash through water and mud, from {{m|enm|plod||a puddle}}. Compare {{cog|nl|plodden}}, {{cog|nl|plodderen}} and {{cog|da|pladder||mire}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|-}}

# A [[slow]] or [[labored]] [[walk]] or other [[motion]] or [[activity]].
#: ''We started at a brisk walk and ended at a '''plod'''.''

====Verb====
{{en-verb|plodd}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[walk]] or [[move]] [[slowly]] and [[heavily]] or [[laboriously]] (+ on, through, over).
#* '''1609''', {{w|William Shakespeare}}, Sonnet 50,<sup>[http://www.opensourceshakespeare.org/views/sonnets/sonnet_view.php?Sonnet=50]</sup>
#*: The beast that bears me, tired with my woe,
#*: '''Plods''' dully on, to bear that weight in me,
#* '''1883''', {{w|Robert Louis Stevenson}}, ''{{w|Treasure Island}}'' Part One, Chapter 1
#** I remember him as if it were yesterday, as he came '''plodding''' to the inn door, his sea chest following behind him in a handbarrow;
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[trudge]] over or through.
#* '''1596''', Henoch Clapham, ''A Briefe of the Bible'', Edinburgh: Robert Walde-grave, p. 127,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A18910.0001.001]</sup>
#*: ''Quest[ion]''. Where was Ioseph?
#*: ''Answ[er]''. It may be, he was playing the Carpenter abrode for all their three livings, but sure it is, he was not idlely '''plodding''' the streetes, much lesse tipling in the Taverne with our idle swingers.
#* '''1799''', {{w|Matthew Gregory Lewis}}, ''The Love of Gain'', London: J. Bell, p. 50, lines 449-451,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004786389.0001.000]</sup>
#*: {{...}} Speed thou to Lombard-street,
#*: Or '''plod''' the gambling 'Change with busy feet,
#*: 'Midst Bulls and Bears some false report to spread,
#* '''1896''', {{w|Alfred Edward Housman|A. E. Housman}}, ''{{w|A Shropshire Lad}}'', London: The Richards Press, XLVI, pp. 69-70,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.73461]</sup>
#*: Break no rosemary, bright with rime
#*: And sparkling to the cruel clime;
#*: Nor '''plod''' the winter land to look
#*: For willows in the icy brook
#*: To cast them leafless round him {{...}}
# To toil; to drudge; especially, to study laboriously and patiently.
#* '''1597''', {{w|Michael Drayton}}, “Edward the fourth to Shores wife” in ''Englands Heroicall Epistles'', London: N. Ling,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A20814.0001.001]</sup>
#*: Poore '''plodding''' schoolemen, they are farre too low,
#*: which by probations, rules and axiom’s goe,
#*: He must be still familiar with the skyes,
#*: which notes the reuolutions of thine eyes;

